# --- registry -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by setup/lib/models.sh; passed to llama-server as environment and
# ignored there. MODEL_SWA=yes means the profile MUST carry --swa-full —
# without it every Claude Code turn re-processes the whole prompt.
MODEL_TITLE=Qwen3.8-Flash-Next · RUNS, text only · 180B UD-Q4_K_XL · 80.4 GiB in GTT at 65536 · tight on a 124.9 GiB machine · needs the PR #27742 build
MODEL_SWA=no
# MODEL_SOURCE — where this model comes from, so the profile can fetch it.
# `<repo> <pattern>…`: every pattern is a substring of a path in that repo and
# every matching .gguf is fetched, so a sharded quant comes down whole and an
# mmproj rides along. Read by setup/get-model.sh, which is this stack's
# equivalent of `ollama pull` — except that the list it offers is MEASURED on
# this hardware (bash setup/lib/models.sh table) rather than a generic catalog.
# Verified 26.08. by matching the exact filename in the repo, not by guessing.
MODEL_SOURCE=unsloth/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GGUF UD-Q4_K_XL
# MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN — measured by the TWO-POINT method, which is the
# accurate one: load at two windows and take the difference, so the weights
# and the compute buffers cancel instead of being estimated. Read by
# setup/lib/budget.py.
# MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB — what GTT shows for this model with the KV taken OUT:
# the weights the GPU pins plus the compute buffers. This is the only profile
# here that needs the field, and it is why the field exists: llama.cpp keeps
# the n-gram table out of GTT ("mmap PLE offload"), so 103.7 GiB of file
# becomes 78.1 GiB of GTT.
#
# It is an OBSERVATION, so setup/lib/budget.py does not add its compute-buffer
# slack on top of it — that would count the buffers twice, which on a model
# this size is 8 GiB and the difference between "tight" and "refused".
MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB=78.1
# MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB — what stays resident OUTSIDE GTT. Declared rather than
# derived, because the obvious derivation is wrong in the dangerous direction:
# file minus GTT is 103.7 - 87.4 = 16.3 GiB at 262144, and the measurement is
# 27.1. It is ANONYMOUS memory (RssAnon 27.1 GiB, Private_Dirty 28.1, no .gguf
# mapping at all), so it is not page cache and not reclaimable.
MODEL_HOST_ANON_GIB=27.1
# MODEL_LAZY_GIB — the part of the file that is NOT resident, because
# llama.cpp reads it row by row from the mapping. OBSERVED, and the record of
# how it was observed is below, because two of the three configurations tried
# on 28.08. do not deliver it and both of them look like they should.
#
# MEASURED 28.08.2026, three runs, same model, same profile, `-c 8192
# -cram 0`, probe read straight after /slots answered:
#
#   build            load_mode   lazy?   RssAnon    peak RSS   result
#   b10665-1 master  none        no      27.13 G    28.06 G    no relief
#   b10665-1 master  mmap        YES     -          -          no /slots after
#                                                              7 min: the whole
#                                                              model loads
#                                                              through the
#                                                              mapping
#   b10665-3 +27837  none        YES      0.31 G     1.27 G    up in 19 s
#
# 27.13 -> 0.31 GiB. The table is the difference and it is 26.82 GiB, which is
# what this field now holds. GTT was 78.44, against the 78.1 measured for
# MODEL_GTT_BASE_GIB — the two agree, so the relief comes off the host side
# only, which is the side that was refusing this profile.
#
# WHY THE MIDDLE ROW IS NOT THE ANSWER. `--load-mode mmap` does enable the
# lazy read, but it mmaps EVERYTHING: buffers stood after 33 s instead of 4,
# and the server was still reading 7 minutes later. #27837 is what separates
# the two — it maps the lazy tensor alone and leaves the weights on the fast
# path. Its own comment names this case: "read_lazy also requires mmap; this
# condition make sure it's usable even when --load-mode is not set to mmap".
#
# WHY THE TOP ROW LOOKS LIKE IT SHOULD WORK AND DOES NOT. The flag defaults to
# `auto`, but the LOADER resolves auto to `none` here: ggml-cuda.cu:4845 sets
# `mmap_support = props->type != IGPU`, the type comes from `prop.integrated`
# (:4819), and the 8060S is integrated. One device without mmap turns it off
# for the whole model (llama-model.cpp:1402), and TENSOR_READ_LAZY requires
# it. Note this is NOT something our hip-integrated-off.patch reaches: that
# one sets `info.devices[id].integrated`, a different copy of the flag.
#
# STILL OPEN. The probe ran before any generation, so the rows had not been
# faulted in yet. What the table costs under real load — how much of it stays
# resident as reclaimable page cache — is not measured. RssFile was 0.07 GiB
# at that moment and can only go up.
#
# budget.lazy_relief() hands this to the guard only if LLAMA_BIN is still the
# build below. A figure observed on one build is not evidence about another,
# and #27837 is an OPEN pull request — if it changes, this has to be measured
# again.
MODEL_LAZY_GIB=26.82
#
# The tensor itself, for the record: per_layer_token_embd.weight, IQ4_NL,
# 26.82 GiB, tagged in src/models/qwen4exp.cpp:140 and lazy above the 4 GiB
# threshold at src/llama-model-loader.cpp:1292. The next largest tensor in the
# model is 0.83 GiB, so this one table is the whole question.
#
# This field was first filled from that tensor size alone, before any run. The
# guard then granted 26.82 GiB, the b10665-1 build delivered none of it, and
# the machine finished that run at 7.1 GiB available with 140 MiB of swap. A
# size says what COULD be demand-paged. Only a run says what is.
MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN=37.3
MODEL_KV_SOURCE=26.08.2026 22:00, by loading at -c 65536 and at -c 262144 and taking the difference: 37.3 KiB per token, 9.3 GiB at 262144. The estimate made before the download had been 24 KiB per token, 56 % low.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ############################################################################
# #  THIS PROFILE CANNOT START YET — and that is deliberate.                 #
# #                                                                          #
# #  -m points at a file that does not exist. setup/waitformodel runs as     #
# #  ExecStartPre and fails on it, so the service refuses instead of         #
# #  serving something wrong, and switch-model.sh stops in preflight with    #
# #  "the model file is not readable". Replace exactly the two lines marked  #
# #  REPLACE and the profile is live.                                        #
# ############################################################################
#
# Qwen3.8-Flash-Next — a preview of the Qwen4 architecture.
# Release: 26.08.2026, 23:00 Beijing = 17:00 CEST, ModelScope and Hugging Face.
#
# WHAT IS CONFIRMED (Hugging Face model page, ModelScope, several outlets):
#   open-weight, multimodal MoE; two new pieces — GDN hybrid layers (Gated
#   Delta Network: linear attention with a fixed-size recurrent state instead
#   of a growing KV cache) and QSA, "Qwen Sparse Attention".
#
# WHAT IS RUMOUR (X posts, repeated by Decrypt/KuCoin/Yahoo; orcarouter marks
# it as speculation — treat every number below as unverified until the config
# is on disk):
#   125B total / 6B active, plus 51B of N-gram embeddings. The N-gram part is
#   a trained lookup table: the last few tokens are hashed, a handful of
#   vectors are fetched and mixed into the hidden state.
#
# THE BLOCKER, as of 26.08. 18:05: llama.cpp cannot load this. Four separate
# pieces are new — the architecture itself, the PLE n-gram embeddings, the QSA
# indexer, and hyper-connections (hc_count 4 on all 48 layers). GDN and
# 512-expert MoE it already speaks; the other four are not an afternoon of
# work upstream.
#
# THE PULL REQUEST IS #27742, not #27739. JJJYmmm opened #27739 "model : add
# Qwen4-Exp" and closed it UNMERGED at 13:56 the same day in favour of
# danielhanchen's #27742 "model: add Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (qwen4exp)", which is
# where the work now is — 20 commits by 15:35, master does not have it yet:
#     bash setup/scripts/build-llama.sh --ref pr/27742
#
# THREE NAMES, and they are not interchangeable. config.json says
# `Qwen4ExpForConditionalGeneration` (that is what the converter registers)
# with `model_type: qwen4_exp`; the GGUF's general.architecture and the entry
# in LLM_ARCH_NAMES are `qwen4exp`, WITHOUT the underscore. Read out of the
# published UD-Q4_K_XL, confirmed against the PR. Grepping for the wrong one
# reports "not supported" forever.
#
# PUBLISHED, and this is the good news of the evening. unsloth went from one
# quant to seven between 17:42 and 18:02 on 26.08.:
#
#     UD-Q4_K_XL   103.7 GiB  4 parts   ~110.2 in GTT of 116 — FITS
#     UD-IQ4_XS     87.2 GiB  3 parts
#     UD-Q3_K_XL    83.8 GiB  3 parts
#     UD-IQ3_XXS    76.3 GiB  3 parts
#     UD-Q2_K_XL    73.5 GiB  3 parts   below the quality floor
#     UD-IQ1_S/M    67.6/69.4 GiB       ~3.0 bpw, below the floor
#
# The prediction before the download was 101-115 GiB for a Q4. It came
# in at 103.7 on disk. Everything after this sentence about how much of that
# lands in GTT was a PREDICTION and it was wrong in both directions — see
# the measured block further down: 87.4 GiB at the full 262144 window, with
# 20 GiB spare against the 108 GiB cap.
#     bash setup/scripts/fetch-model.sh unsloth/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GGUF \
#          UD-Q4_K_XL
# ~3 hours at the 10 MiB/s measured here, and resumable. Nothing can LOAD it
# until #27742 lands, so fetching early costs only disk.
#
# --- the memory arithmetic, MEASURED after the release ---------------------
#
# Read out of the repository itself on 26.08. 14:30 — config.json, the weight
# index, and the safetensors header of all 131 shards. 335.3 GiB bf16 = 180 B
# parameters, and they sit here:
#
#   MoE experts        229.7 GiB  68.5 %   512 experts, 10 active per token
#   n-gram table        95.4 GiB  28.4 %   128 tensors, each [2500012 x 160]
#   MTP head             4.9 GiB   1.4 %   -> speculation is possible
#   linear attention     3.9 GiB   1.2 %   36 of 48 layers (GDN)
#   embed / lm_head      2.4 GiB   0.7 %
#   hyper-connections    1.2 GiB   0.4 %   hc_count 4, all 48 layers
#   full attn + QSA      1.1 GiB   0.3 %   12 of 48 layers, indexer_budget 2048
#   vision tower         0.8 GiB   0.2 %   27 blocks
#
# At a realistic Q4 (~4.5 bpw): experts 69 GiB + table 29 GiB + rest 3 GiB
# = 101 GiB of weights. KV is the cheap part and that is the hybrid paying
# off — only 12 layers keep a real cache, 2 KV heads x 256 head_dim, ~24 KiB
# per token, so 6.3 GiB at the FULL 262144 context (qwen38 needs 13.2 at 200k).
#
#   101 GiB weights + 6.3 GiB KV + buffers  ~=  110 of the cap   <- PREDICTED
#    78 GiB weights + 9.3 GiB KV            ~=   87.4            <- MEASURED
#
# It fits, with ~15 GiB for the host — and only because GTT was raised from 96
# to 116 this morning. At 96 it would not have.
#
# THE NUMBER THAT DECIDES IT was the quantisation of the N-GRAM TABLE — left
# at Q8 it is 54 GiB instead of 29, the total 127 GiB, and nothing fits.
# ANSWERED, 26.08. 18:04, read out of shard 2 of the published UD-Q4_K_XL:
#
#     per_layer_token_embd.weight   160 x 320001536   IQ4_NL   ~26.8 GiB
#
# 4.5 bits per weight, not 8. unsloth did the right thing and the model fits
# because of it. Re-check this in any OTHER quant before trusting its size.
#
#   1. GTT is at 108 GiB (bash setup/scripts/gtt.sh --verify). It was 116
#      for one day; the measured footprint is 87.4, so the extra 8 bought
#      nothing and cost the ceiling. 20 GiB of headroom remain.
#
#   2. -ot on the n-gram table: NOT NEEDED. Measured 26.08. 22:00 — llama.cpp
#      already keeps the table out of GTT. 103.7 GiB of file becomes 78.1 GiB
#      of weights in GTT, and adding
#        -ot "per_layer_token_embd=CPU"
#      changes nothing: 80.4 GiB either way, byte for byte.
#
#      That result on its own was ambiguous — a pattern that matches nothing
#      looks identical to a placement that has already happened. The
#      discriminator settled it: -ot "blk\.[0-9]+\.ffn_.*=CPU" moves the
#      experts and GTT falls from 80.4 to 9.1 GiB with VmRSS at 100.4. So the
#      flag works in this build, and the no-op means the table was already
#      where we wanted it.
#
#      Keep the name anyway: per_layer_token_embd, one tensor, IQ4_NL. NOT
#      "ple" — blk.1.ple_key, ple_value, ple_conv1d and the three ple_norm_*
#      are small projections that belong on the GPU.
#      NOT as a way to create memory: it moves the same bytes from one side of
#      the cap to the other. What it buys is RESIDENCY — a CPU-buffer tensor
#      WAS EXPECTED TO keep the GGUF mmap (llama-model.cpp:1700; the CPU
#      device advertises
#      buffer_from_host_ptr, the HIP device does not), so only the pages
#      actually touched would be resident and evictable. 320 M rows of
#      trigrams, of which a session touches a few thousand. In GTT every byte
#      is copied in and pinned. Nobody has run that experiment.
#      NOT --cpu-moe: that moves the EXPERT weights, the one genuinely
#      bandwidth-bound part.
#
# --- what is NOT open, and must not be re-litigated tonight ---------------
#
# -np 1 and the PATCHED ROCm binary. Both defects behind that are properties
# of gfx1151, not of Qwen 3.8 27B, so they apply to this model from the first
# start (measured 26.08., bench/suites/restore-safety.py):
#   1) the gfx1151 HIP race — the stock build corrupts with two slots even
#      with an empty prefix store (llama.cpp #27579, #27506);
#   2) slot restore with two slots poisons the context.
# Patched + 2 slots was clean on one server start and corrupt on the next.
# One slot was clean in every cell. Correctness beats throughput.
#
# f16 KV, deliberately: the one q8_0-KV cell measured (24.08.,
# rocm-medium-spec-q8kv) dropped the long-context task that f16 solved.
# A SECOND reason arrived on 26.08. from PR #27742: quantised KV does not
# merely cost quality on this architecture, it CRASHES —
# `GGML_ASSERT(inp->self_k_rot == nullptr && inp->self_v_rot == nullptr)` at
# qwen4exp.cpp:544, reported for both q8_0 and q4_0. The workaround offered
# there is LLAMA_ATTN_ROT_DISABLE=1 or f16. We were on f16 for a quality
# reason and it turns out to also be the only one that runs.
#
# ROCm, not Vulkan — IF the model turns out to have an MTP head. Measured
# 24.08.: with MTP/ngram speculation Vulkan halves the decode (96.9 vs 51.7 s
# median). Without speculation Vulkan wins. So: check for a draft/MTP head
# first (nextn_predict_layers in the config), and if there is none, measure
#     python3 bench/run.py --env setup/env/flashnext.env
# against build-vulkan before settling.
#
# --- fill in tonight ------------------------------------------------------
#
#   [x] -m points at the real file since 26.08. 21:30 — part one of four,
#       which finds the other three. All four verified against Hugging Face's
#       sha256 (setup/scripts/fetch-model.sh checks it).
#   [x] --mmproj REMOVED, not left pointing at a missing file: there is no
#       mmproj in the unsloth repo, and llama-server treats an unreadable one
#       as fatal. The vision tower is 0.8 GiB of a checkpoint nobody has
#       converted. Text only until somebody does.
#   [x] MODEL_SWA=no — confirmed from the config: no sliding_window key. The
#       12 full-attention layers use QSA (a learned index picking 2048 tokens),
#       which is not a window, and the other 36 are linear attention.
#   [x] -c 65536, NOT the full window — and the difference between those two
#       sentences is a machine hang.
#
#       262144 LOADS. It was measured, it works, GTT 87.4 GiB. It was set here
#       on the strength of that, and the next run took the box down. "It
#       loads" and "it loads with room left to live in" are two different
#       claims and they were conflated:
#
#           -c  65536   80.4 GTT + ~25 anon = 106 GiB -> 18.9 left
#           -c 262144   87.4 GTT + ~25 anon = 113 GiB -> 11.9 left
#
#       A desktop session uses more than 11.9. The 7 GiB of KV that the full
#       window reserves buys capacity nothing here reaches — the deepest probe
#       in bench/ is 32768 tokens — and costs the margin the machine lives on.
#
#       And a correction to what this file said an hour earlier: the ~25 GiB
#       that never enters GTT is NOT reclaimable page cache. The kernel's OOM
#       report for the killed server reads anon-rss:25848732kB — it is
#       ANONYMOUS memory, evictable only to swap, and swap here is 4 GiB of
#       zram. CONFIRMED 27.08. by reading a live server rather than an OOM
#       report: RssAnon 27.1 GiB, RssFile 0.12, Private_Dirty 28.1, no
#       .gguf mapping in smaps. This paragraph was right and the mmap
#       paragraph above it was the theory.
#       zram. That mistake is why the arithmetic looked survivable.
#
#       Measured 26.08. 22:00 by loading at 65536 and at 262144 and taking the
#       difference:
#
#           weights (no KV)   78.1 GiB
#           KV                37.3 KiB per token  -> 9.3 GiB at 262144
#           GTT at 262144     87.4 GiB of 116, 28.6 GiB spare
#
#       The estimate made before the download was 24 KiB per token, 56 %
#       low — and it did not matter, because the same estimate was 30 GiB
#       PESSIMISTIC about the weights. Two errors in opposite directions is
#       luck, not accuracy.
#   [x] CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=200000 in setup/claude/local.json now
#       fits inside the window; switch-model.sh's preflight warning is gone.
#   [ ] speculation: the MTP head EXISTS in the checkpoint (4.9 GiB, one
#       layer), but it is converted to a SEPARATE gguf — `convert_hf_to_gguf
#       --mtp`, passed as `-md mtp-….gguf` — and unsloth has not published
#       one. So --spec-type draft-mtp is not available from this repo yet.
#       Note also that this model has n-gram machinery of its OWN, which is a
#       different thing from the ngram-mod drafter.
#   [ ] AUTO_SAVE=0 in ~/.config/cc-gateway.env before this profile serves —
#       see the note under LLAMA_ARGS. Revert it together with
#       --slot-save-path once restore-safety has run green.
#   [ ] MODEL_TITLE: drop the "NOT READY" once it is.
#
# --- two arguments that differ from every other profile here ------------
#
# -fit off. `-fit` defaults to ON (common/common.h:468, fit_params = true), so
#   we get it without asking. It adjusts unset memory arguments to what it
#   thinks fits — and it cannot model this architecture: a report on #27742
#   (26.08., RTX 3090, UD-IQ1_S) shows the memory breakdown printing
#   `unaccounted -47656 MiB` and then dying in `common_params_fit_impl` with
#   `GGML_ASSERT(obj_new)` in ggml.c. That is the generic fit machinery, not
#   a CUDA path, so different hardware is no protection. And we have no use
#   for it: every memory parameter here is set by hand, which is the entire
#   point of gtt.sh and of these profiles.
#
# NO --slot-save-path, and that is a removal, not an omission. Two reviewers
#   independently flagged save/restore on #27742:
#     ggerganov: "the implementation is missing state save/load for the
#       indexer — saving/restoring checkpoints will probably not work?"
#     ngxson   : "'predecessors' should better be part of the llama_memory,
#       otherwise a context save/load will corrupt it"
#   The QSA indexer carries state that the restore path does not know about.
#   What makes this worse than a crash is the failure MODE: a poisoned
#   restore does not raise, it degrades the output — this repo has the
#   incident of 25.08. on record. So the flag stays out until
#   `python3 bench/suites/restore-safety.py` has run against THIS model.
#   Nobody has run it.
#
#   What it costs, precisely: -cram 32768 is a DIFFERENT mechanism and stays.
#   The RAM-backed prompt cache still catches an evicted prefix inside one
#   server run. What is gone is persistence ACROSS a restart, and prefixes
#   for projects that have not been touched since the server came up.
#
#   And it has a consequence at the other end: cc-gateway auto-saves every
#   warmed prefix. Against a server without --slot-save-path that save fails,
#   is caught and logged as "NOTE automatic save … failed" — but the id never
#   enters SAVED, so it is retried on every cold prefix, one prewarm
#   subprocess each time. Set AUTO_SAVE=0 in ~/.config/cc-gateway.env while
#   this profile serves. It is in the checklist above.
# -cram 4096, NOT 32768, and that correction is worth its paragraph.
#
# `-cram` is MiB (common/arg.cpp: "set the maximum cache size in MiB"), so
# 32768 is a standing promise of 32 GiB of RAM prompt cache — and it was copied
# here from qwen38.env, where the memory story is completely different. This
# model already holds 25.6 GiB of host memory that never enters GTT. Measured
# 27.08. while it ran:
#
#     GTT                80.0 GiB
#     RssAnon            27.1 GiB     ANONYMOUS, not page cache
#     RssFile             0.12 GiB    essentially nothing is file-backed
#     Private_Dirty      28.1 GiB     not reclaimable
#     total              109.6 of 124.9, with 10 GiB available
#
# Add 32 GiB of prompt cache on top and the host side reaches ~57 GiB, the
# total ~137, and the machine has 124.9. It has never been hit only because
# every run so far was short enough that the cache never filled — on qwen38,
# which carries the same 32768, RssAnon went from 0.6 to 12.5 GiB in one
# morning of probing.
#
# 4096 is ample for anything measured here: the deepest probe is ~36k tokens,
# and at the measured 37.3 KiB per token that is 1.3 GiB per prefix.
#
# --chat-template-kwargs, and it is not cosmetic either. This template
# defaults to reasoning_effort 'xhigh' when enable_thinking is unset (read out
# of the GGUF, 27.08.), so a probe gets a THINKING answer — which is how the
# copy workload's self-check fired on 27.08. with "the model answered in the
# thinking channel". Same key and same trap as qwen38: the template accepts
# xhigh/medium/low only, and 'none' raises a Jinja exception.
# --- reasoning modes ------------------------------------------------------
#
# The rule and the vocabulary: setup/claude/modes.py. The two lines below are
# GENERATED — `python3 bench/suites/effort-vocabulary.py --profile flashnext` —
# and everything model-specific about them is the measurement that follows.
#
# MEASURED 28.08.2026. This template renders three levels — low, medium and
# xhigh — and aliases `high` to xhigh itself. `max` and `none` RAISE, an HTTP
# 500 at the server, so max is sent as xhigh: the highest thing this template
# actually has.
#
# high, xhigh and max therefore all SEND xhigh, and that is deliberate. They
# render identically (sha 546e0307), so sending the consumer's own word would
# make three different chat_template_kwargs for one prompt — and since the
# kwargs entered the prefix id, three cache keys and two wasted prefills.
#
# `on+` sets BOTH knobs, and it is not decoration: this template gates the
# effort block on enable_thinking, and LLAMA_ARGS below switches that off. A
# mode sending only the level renders identically to sending nothing (measured:
# 1ad7792b either way) while carrying its own prefix id.
#
# No `none` mode: the bare alias already means "what the command line says",
# and that is thinking off. A `none` here would render 1ad7792b too — the same
# prompt under a second cache key. An unoffered name falls through to the bare
# alias and renders the same thing anyway, so leaving it out costs nothing.
MODES=low:on+low  medium:on+medium  high:on+high  xhigh:on+high  max:on+high
#
# TEMPLATE_LEVELS is the measurement, not a policy: what this template renders.
# It exists so a mode declaring something else — `max:on+max` looks
# symmetrical and is a 500 — is refused at load instead of once per request.
TEMPLATE_LEVELS=low  medium  high  xhigh
#
LLAMA_ARGS=--alias flashnext -m @MODELS@/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-UD-Q4_K_XL-00001-of-00004.gguf \
  -ngl 999 -fa on -fit off -ub 2048 -b 2048 -c 65536 -np 1 -cram 4096 \
  --chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":false} \
  --jinja --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080

# NOT the stable symlink. It is a PINNED path, and that is deliberate: a
# profile that followed `build-rocm-patched` would silently change backend
# under a rebuild. Everything else here keeps serving from the build that has
# been measured.
#
# UNTIL 28.08. this pointed at the build of PR #27742, because qwen4exp was
# not on master. It is now (merged 27.08., commit 6c84c7d5d), so that reason
# has expired — but a second one replaced it.
#
# The pin is now #27837, an OPEN pull request, cherry-picked onto
# master + our gfx1151 patch:
#
#   ca3d5a3e1  origin/master, 28.08.
#   d788341c6  gfx1151: do not trust prop.integrated on HIP   (ours)
#   8434b82f6  force lazy tensor on cpu if lazy is on         ) #27837
#   c93d460b6  llama: improve TENSOR_READ_LAZY handling       )
#
# WITHOUT those last two this model does not fit on this machine. The whole
# argument is at MODEL_LAZY_GIB: on master the PLE table is loaded into 27 GiB
# of anonymous memory because `auto` resolves to `none` on an iGPU, and with
# #27837 it is demand-paged and RssAnon is 0.31. budget.lazy_relief() checks
# that this exact path is what runs before it subtracts anything, so changing
# this line withdraws the relief and the guard refuses the profile again —
# which is the correct behaviour, not a regression.
#
# It is an open PR. If it is rewritten before it merges, rebuild and MEASURE
# AGAIN rather than assuming the figure carried over.
LLAMA_BIN=llama.cpp/build-rocm-patched-b10665-3-gc93d460b6/bin/llama-server
